+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes a new service for larder, or weighs one in review. It holds what the team favors when two good
+options conflict. Written in October 2026 after issue 70, the third proposal that year to add a service, so the next proposal starts
+from the rule instead of rediscovering it.
+
+## Tenets
+
+### Add a service only for what Postgres cannot do, and only with two volunteers to restore it
+
+Larder runs one Postgres database and its own code, the web app and its worker. Rules out a service that does a job
+Postgres can already do, and a service only one volunteer could restore. It does not rule out a service larder does
+not run: the card gateway is outside the repository, and a hosted service nobody has to run is acceptable.
+
+The rule comes from services that only one volunteer could run. In 2023 Redis was proposed for sessions and turned
+down for that reason. In March 2025 Redis did run, for Celery's queue: it evicted 300 receipt jobs one Saturday night,
+and only one volunteer could restore it. Both times Postgres did the job. In October 2026, issue 70 proposed
+Elasticsearch because searching the pantry for "rice" took four seconds and missed "Rice, brown"; Postgres full-text
+search on the item name answered in 40 ms and found it, and the proposal moved to that.
+
+Applied in [keep sessions in the database](adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) and
+[run background jobs on Postgres](adrs/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md).
